                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_condition:     `<strong>Conditional is the disciplined call on the flagship.</strong> 'Climate Positive' can earn its place — but only behind full third-party verification. Run it before the proof lands and you've handed the watchdog its headline.`,
                    coach_r1_defensible:    `<strong>Calling it 'Defensible' as-is is exactly the maximalist trap.</strong> The reforestation programme is real but the verification is incomplete — that's a Conditional, not a green light. The watchdog needs minutes, not days, to find the gap.`,
                    coach_r1_drop:          `<strong>Dropping the flagship outright forfeits your best story.</strong> Authenticity isn't silence. The claim is salvageable with proof — over-correcting to nothing is its own failure, and your investor will ask why a sustainability brand won't talk about sustainability.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp claims audit.</strong> Conditional on the flagship AND qualifying the absolutes — '94% plant-based', 'plastic-free bottle' — is precisely the move: specific, verifiable claims keep the story and disarm the seven sins.`,
                    coach_r1_keep:          `<strong>Punchy absolutes are the fastest route to a fine.</strong> '100% natural' and 'plastic-free' are false the moment a refill cap is 6% fossil plastic. Vagueness and fibbing are two of the seven sins — qualify them precisely or drop the word 'plastic-free'.`,
                    coach_r1_dropall:       `<strong>Saying nothing at all is over-correction.</strong> You can make a specific, true claim — '94% plant-based' — instead of either an absolute or silence. Scrubbing your ingredient and packaging story forfeits the very thing customers pay the premium for.`,
                    coach_r1_noproof:       `<strong>You've gone Conditional but barely funded the proof.</strong> 'Conditional on verification' only works if you actually buy the verification. With this little ring-fenced, you won't be able to substantiate when the challenge lands in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r2_owned:         `<strong>An owned/earned-led mix protects the asset.</strong> Content, PR and in-store proof grow slower than TV but they let you show the evidence rather than just shout the claim — credibility compounds instead of eroding.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:     `<strong>A workable balance.</strong> You kept enough owned/earned to protect credibility while buying real reach. Watch the mass share — every euro of TV behind a fragile claim multiplies the exposure.`,
                    coach_r2_mass:          `<strong>A mass-reach-heavy mix amplifies whatever risk your claims carry.</strong> TV and retail media buy the fastest lift — and the widest blast radius if a claim is fragile. With thin owned/earned, you're shouting a claim you can't show.`,
                    coach_r2_listterms:     `<strong>Entering on your own terms keeps the promise intact.</strong> You protected the price and the recyclable packaging, so the supermarket shelf reinforces the brand instead of contradicting it. Volume is smaller, credibility is whole.`,
                    coach_r2_concede:       `<strong>You took the listing at −12% in plastic multipacks.</strong> Volume is up — and a shopper just photographed your 'plastic-free' brand wrapped in plastic on a discount shelf. You've put your premium and your promise on the same line; which one breaks first?`,
                    coach_r2_decline:       `<strong>Declining protects the brand but leaves growth on the table.</strong> Defensible if the terms truly break the promise — but the board still wants +18%, so the reach has to come from somewhere you can defend.`,
                    coach_r3_infldecline:   `<strong>Right call on the celebrity.</strong> 4.1M followers and a fast-fashion feed would have rented you a credibility gap — 'since when does she care about the planet?' is the first comment you'd never live down. Reach without fit is a liability.`,
                    coach_r3_inflaccept:    `<strong>You bought reach and rented a credibility gap.</strong> The first comment under the launch post: 'since when does she care about the planet?' A poor-fit megaphone amplifies doubt, not belief — what it did to impressions it undid in your values segment.`,
                    coach_r3_microswap:     `<strong>On-values creators are the smarter trade.</strong> Less raw reach, but authentic fit means the endorsement reinforces your story instead of undercutting it. Borrowed credibility only works when it's credible.`,
                    coach_r3_cobrand:       `<strong>The eco-label lends verified, hard-to-fake credibility.</strong> 30% of control and margin is a real price — but a built, established label reinforces authentic commitment rather than renting a halo. Worth it if your own proof is thin.`,
                    coach_r3_disclose:      `<strong>Imperfect-but-disclosed beats perfect-but-secret.</strong> Publishing your 92%-traceable data and owning the 8% gap is the hardest endorsement to fake — and it pre-empts the watchdog's 'what are they hiding?' story before it can start.`,
                    coach_r3_decline:       `<strong>Declining the transparency demand reads as concealment.</strong> The NGO will publish that you declined — and '92% isn't perfect, so we hid it' becomes 'what are they hiding?'. Selective transparency is the story you least want.`,
                    coach_r3_spin:          `<strong>A spin-led pre-draft sets you up to fail the stress-test.</strong> The watchdog will ask an evidence question; a holding line built on warm words instead of proof will accelerate the crisis when the 72 hours are up. Make it proof-led now.`,
                    coach_r4_substantiate:  `<strong>You answered an evidence challenge with evidence.</strong> Because you ring-fenced the proof in Round 1, you could publish third-party verification and stand 'Climate Positive' up — the watchdog's thread collapses and your credibility climbs. This is the whole game.`,
                    coach_r4_nosub:         `<strong>You tried to substantiate a claim you never funded the proof for.</strong> Saying 'here's our verification' with no verification is the worst of both worlds — it reads as a bluff. The time to buy the proof was Round 1; now you can only reframe or withdraw.`,
                    coach_r4_reframe:       `<strong>Reframing to a claim you can fully prove is the honest recovery.</strong> Drop the fragile absolute, switch to the specific verifiable version, and the evidence question now has an evidence answer. You keep the story and disarm the challenge.`,
                    coach_r4_partial:       `<strong>A partial withdrawal limits the damage but isn't decisive.</strong> Pulling only the contested claim stops the bleeding, yet leaves the impression you were caught rather than candid. A proof-led substantiation or a clean reframe lands stronger.`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>Defending an unsubstantiated claim hard is the cardinal error.</strong> 'We stand firmly behind our commitment' to an evidence question is a communications answer to a substance problem — it pours fuel on the crisis and the regulator's file thickens.`,
                    coach_r4_fullpull:      `<strong>Full withdrawal is safe but admits the whole campaign was built on sand.</strong> You eliminate the legal risk and forfeit nearly all the lift — the board's +18% is gone, and the brand looks like it couldn't tell a true story from a false one. There was a claim you could have kept.`,
                    coach_r4_pr:            `<strong>A values statement to an evidence question accelerates the crisis.</strong> 'We stand firmly behind our commitment to the planet' is exactly the answer the journalist wants — because their next line is 'so that's a no on the verification?'. Answer with proof or an honest reframe.`,
                    coach_r4_surfaced:      `<strong>The stress-test surfaced the exposure your launch had hidden.</strong> You never funded the substantiation in Round 1, so the contested claim has no proof behind it — and now the watchdog has found exactly that. A partial fix won't fully close a gap you chose not to fund; only a clean reframe or real verification does.`,
                    coach_r4_fined:         `<strong>The DGCCRF acted — a fine of up to 80% of your campaign spend.</strong> The standing greenwashing risk was too high when the challenge landed, so the regulator could move. The extra reach from the fragile claims never paid for the exposure they created.`,

